# Minimal environment for every zsh invocation, including `ssh host 'cmd'`.
#
# Architecture note for future agents:
# - `ssh host 'cmd'` with a zsh login shell runs a non-interactive command shell.
#   zsh reads .zshenv there, but skips .zprofile and .zshrc.
# - Remote bootstrap tools such as Codex App SSH connections need standalone user
#   CLIs like ~/.local/bin/codex to be visible even in that thin environment.
# - Keep this file silent and cheap. No Homebrew `shellenv`, plugin managers,
#   prompts, completions, timeouts, or commands that may print or hang.
# - ~/.bun/bin is bun's global bin dir: `bun add -g` CLIs (claude, gemini, …) and this
#   repo's own PATH commands (package.json `bin`, installed by `bun link` from `mise run
#   deps`). It belongs on EVERY shell's PATH for the same reason ~/.local/bin does — a
#   hook, an agent, or `ssh host 'agent-resource-run …'` runs in exactly this thin
#   environment. Until 2026-09-13 only .zprofile added it, so those commands existed in a
#   terminal and vanished one process down. ~/.local/bin stays FIRST: official standalone
#   CLIs there (codex) must beat any stale npm/Bun shim of the same name.
#
# ── NO IMPLICIT GLOBAL TOOLCHAIN (INV-6) ─────────────────────────────────────
# A managed tool is reachable where a config DECLARES it, or not at all. There is no
# global default version anywhere: `~/.config/mise/config.toml` declares no [tools], and
# nothing here may reintroduce one.
#
# mise ships TWO delivery paths and they are not interchangeable:
#   `mise activate` (zsh/zshrc) — per-directory, declaration-driven → INTERACTIVE shells
#   the shim dir    (below)     — one static dir holding a name for EVERY installed
#                                 binary of EVERY installed version → NON-INTERACTIVE only
# This file used to add the shim dir unconditionally, so both ran at once and every
# installed tool squatted a name on the interactive PATH. What leaked was the NAME, not a
# version: the shim resolves the version at exec time and, finding no declaration, refuses.
# Measured 2026-08-06 — `which npm` answered with a path while `npm -V` died with
# "No version is set for shim: npm". A name that exists and cannot run.
#
# The shim dir stays for the non-interactive case because that is the one thing it is
# load-bearing for: `ssh host 'cmd'` skips .zshrc, so `mise activate` never runs and a
# DECLARED tool would otherwise be unreachable. Measured in that exact environment:
#   cd ~          → `bun --version` = "No version is set for shim" (correctly refuses)
#   cd ~/dotfiles → `bun --version` = 1.3.14                        (mise.toml declares it)
# Both halves are asserted by `mise run test:mise-scope`.
#
# Corollary, enforced by the same test: no second version manager may hook a login shell.
# fnm was removed 2026-08-06 for exactly this reason — it held no node yet ran `fnm env`
# on every login, prepending a PATH entry no config asked for (and leaving 1672 stale
# multishell dirs behind).

# ── LOCALE SAFETY NET (2026-09-13, root-caused) ──────────────────────────────
# Something upstream of this shell exports LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 — not this repo: searched
# zsh/*, wsl/*, herdr/config.toml, and every system-level /etc/{profile,zsh,locale.conf,
# default/locale} on this WSL host, none set LANG/LC_ALL. Whatever sets it (Windows
# Terminal profile env, WSL init — outside this repo's reach) assumes en_US.UTF-8 is
# installed; on this host `locale -a` only has C / C.utf8 / POSIX. glibc's setlocale()
# then fails (the "setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale" warning every subshell
# prints), and falls back to the C locale for width-aware string handling — which is why
# `git log --graph` (CJK commit subjects next to ASCII graph lines), `less`, and `column`
# render garbled even though the underlying bytes are correct UTF-8. The real fix is
# `sudo locale-gen en_US.UTF-8` at the host that's missing it, which a dotfile can't do
# (needs root, and doing it silently on every shell start would be worse than the bug).
# Normalize instead: if the locale this shell was HANDED isn't actually generated, fall
# back to C.UTF-8 — always present on modern glibc, still fully UTF-8-aware — rather than
# silently running in a broken one. Pure zsh + one `locale -a` call: cheap, no network,
# can't hang.
if [[ -n "${LC_ALL:-$LANG}" ]]; then
  _zshenv_locale="${(L)${LC_ALL:-$LANG}//-/}"
  _zshenv_avail=("${(f)$(locale -a 2>/dev/null)}")
  _zshenv_locale_ok=0
  for _zshenv_l in "${_zshenv_avail[@]}"; do
    [[ "${(L)_zshenv_l//-/}" == "$_zshenv_locale" ]] && { _zshenv_locale_ok=1; break }
  done
  if (( ! _zshenv_locale_ok )); then
    export LANG=C.UTF-8
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
  fi
  unset _zshenv_locale _zshenv_avail _zshenv_locale_ok _zshenv_l
fi

# TELL mise, do not out-edit it. `mise activate` gained a setting, `activate_shims` (default
# TRUE), whose effect is to append the shim dir to PATH from inside activate's own output —
# measured on mise 2026.9.3, the emitted script ends with `export PATH='<shims>:...'`. activate
# runs from .zshrc, i.e. AFTER everything below, so every defence written here was being undone
# one file later and `mise run test:mise-scope` failed on exactly that (18 passed / 1 failed,
# "shim dir LEAKED — some later startup file re-adds it").
#
# The direction of the flow is the whole point. Correcting mise's output downstream is a race
# this file cannot win: mise writes last, so the only durable answer is to stop it CONTRIBUTING,
# which is an input to mise, not an edit of its result. One declaration replaces a defence that
# would otherwise have to grow a new mechanism every time activate changes. Measured, whole gate:
#   as-is                        18 passed / 1 failed
#   MISE_ACTIVATE_SHIMS=false    19 passed / 0 failed
#
# The env var rather than `[settings]` in ~/.config/mise/config.toml, because THIS file already
# owns the question "who puts the shim dir on PATH" — the answer belongs beside the rule it
# qualifies, not in a second file in a second format. It must be exported before .zshrc runs
# activate; .zshenv always does.
#
# What this does NOT do is remove the two guards further down. Both were re-verified by deleting
# them one at a time with this declaration already in place, and each still fails its own case:
#   without the strip loop        18/1  "inherited shim dir survived (strip is missing)"
#   without the ORIG_PATH block   18/1  "shim dir survives in __MISE_ORIG_PATH"
# They defend a different boundary — PROCESS INHERITANCE, where there is no upstream to declare
# to, because a child cannot tell the parent that spawned it what to export. Sanitising what
# arrives at the entry point is the correct shape there, not a symptom of the same race.
export MISE_ACTIVATE_SHIMS=false

_zshenv_shims="$HOME/.local/share/mise/shims"
_zshenv_cand=(
  "$HOME/.local/bin"
  "$HOME/.bun/bin"
  "$_zshenv_shims"
  "$HOME/.cargo/bin"
  "$HOME/.foundry/bin"
)

_zshenv_path_prefix=()
for _zshenv_dir in "${_zshenv_cand[@]}"; do
  [[ "$_zshenv_dir" == "$_zshenv_shims" ]] && [[ -o interactive ]] && continue
  [[ -d "$_zshenv_dir" ]] && _zshenv_path_prefix+=("$_zshenv_dir")
done

# Strip EVERY candidate, not only the ones being re-added. An interactive shell inherits
# PATH from whatever spawned it — the tmux server, an editor, a non-interactive parent —
# so without this the shim dir rides in from the parent and the gate above is decorative.
for _zshenv_dir in "${_zshenv_cand[@]}"; do
  path=("${(@)path:#$_zshenv_dir}")
done
path=($_zshenv_path_prefix $path)

# Second carrier, same leak. `mise activate` stashes the pre-activation PATH in an EXPORTED
# __MISE_ORIG_PATH and reuses an inherited one verbatim (`if [ -z "${__MISE_ORIG_PATH:-}" ]`),
# rebuilding PATH from it on every hook. So an interactive shell spawned from an activated
# NON-interactive parent — Claude Code's Bash tool, an `ssh host 'cmd'` session that then
# starts tmux — inherits a base PATH that still holds the shim dir, and mise restores it one
# prompt after the block above stripped it. Measured 2026-08-06 in a real login pty:
# clean env → no shim dir; env inherited from an activated parent → shim dir back.
# __MISE_DIFF is deliberately left alone: that is how mise undoes the PARENT's tool dirs.
if [[ -o interactive ]] && [[ -n "${__MISE_ORIG_PATH:-}" ]]; then
  _zshenv_orig=("${(@s.:.)__MISE_ORIG_PATH}")
  _zshenv_orig=("${(@)_zshenv_orig:#$_zshenv_shims}")
  export __MISE_ORIG_PATH="${(j.:.)_zshenv_orig}"
  unset _zshenv_orig
fi

typeset -U path
unset _zshenv_dir _zshenv_cand _zshenv_shims _zshenv_path_prefix
export PATH

# ── WSL interop for sshd-spawned shells ──────────────────────────────────────
# Launching ANY Windows binary from WSL2 needs WSL_INTEROP to name a live per-session unix
# socket under /run/WSL/. sshd inherits none, so in every shell that arrives over ssh the
# variable is UNSET and every Windows binary dies with a bare "Invalid argument" — a message
# that names nothing, and sends the reader after PATH or /etc/wsl.conf interop instead.
# Measured on r99 2026-09-13 — non-interactive `ssh host 'cmd'`, `zsh -lc` and an interactive
# login shell were ALL unset, and with it unset:
#   explorer.exe / clip.exe / cmd.exe    ->  rc=1 "Invalid argument"
#   clip.exe with WSL_INTEROP=<live>     ->  rc=0
# That socket is the whole WSL<->Windows bridge, so what was broken is not one tool: cc/pp/pwdc
# (clipboard), o/oo/open (explorer), s/start, e/ee (VS Code launcher) and the wsl:* mise tasks
# all route through it. On r99, ssh is the ONLY way in, so all of them were dead there.
#
# WHY THIS FILE and not zsh/wsl.zsh: wsl.zsh is sourced from aliases.zsh, i.e. interactive
# shells only, while `ssh host 'mise run wsl:wslconfig'` reads nothing but .zshenv. Fixing it
# in wsl.zsh would repair the half that is easiest to notice and leave the automated half —
# the half agents and mise tasks use — still broken.
#
# BEST-EFFORT, and that limit is deliberate rather than overlooked. Socket FILES outlive their
# session: 4 were present on r99 and 2 of them were dead. Liveness cannot be tested without
# spawning a process, which this file must never do, so it takes the NEWEST socket — the most
# recently created session being the one most likely still alive. Tools that need certainty
# retry across candidates themselves (scripts/wsl-audit.ts does, newest-first, up to 4).
# Glob qualifiers: `=` keeps only real sockets, `om` orders by mtime newest-first, `N` makes an
# empty match empty rather than an error.
if [[ -z "${WSL_INTEROP:-}" ]] && [[ -d /run/WSL ]]; then
  _zshenv_socks=(/run/WSL/*_interop(N=om))
  if (( $#_zshenv_socks )); then
    export WSL_INTEROP="${_zshenv_socks[1]}"
  fi
  unset _zshenv_socks
fi

# cocoindex-code: when a supervisor unit owns the daemon, no client may spawn its own.
# Without this, every client (shell, MCP server, agent) that finds the socket missing —
# after a settings edit, a crash, or the idle exit — forks an UNCAPPED daemon into its own
# cgroup, and that daemon outlives it. See cocoindex/ccc-daemon.service.wsl.
# The guard is the unit file itself, not an OS test: no unit -> no supervisor -> stock
# lazy-spawn behavior (macOS), which is what we want there.
# An `if` block, not `test && export`: as the LAST statement, the latter makes .zshenv itself
# exit 1 wherever the unit is absent (macOS), which aborts any later `setopt ERR_EXIT; source`.
if [[ -e "$HOME/.config/systemd/user/ccc-daemon.service" ]]; then
  export COCOINDEX_CODE_DAEMON_SUPERVISED=1
fi

# cocoindex-code: keep each project's index DB OUT of the repo. ccc's default is
# <project>/.cocoindex_code/, where the daemon rewrites GBs on every commit and every tool that
# walks the repo tree pays for derived bytes (firedancer measured 2026-09-23: polysearch landing
# 21–36 s while the in-repo DB was being rewritten, 0.65–0.88 s otherwise). ccc's prefix mapping
# moves a project under $HOME to ~/.cache/cocoindex-code/db/<path relative to $HOME>/; only
# settings.yml stays in the repo. Every CLIENT needs this (`ccc reset`/`ccc status` and
# repo-retrieve's watermark resolve the DB dir client-side); the daemon needs the SAME value —
# lazily spawned (macOS) it inherits it from here, supervised (WSL) it gets it from
# cocoindex/ccc-daemon.service.wsl. A mismatch makes client and daemon open different DBs;
# `repo-retrieve index` refuses to certify one it cannot see, and `mise run doctor` compares both.
export COCOINDEX_CODE_DB_PATH_MAPPING="$HOME=$HOME/.cache/cocoindex-code/db"
